========================================
POSITION: ABSOLUTE IN CSS
========================================

DEFINITION:

position: absolute removes an element from
the normal document flow and positions it
relative to its nearest positioned ancestor
using top, right, bottom, and left.

If no positioned ancestor exists, it positions
relative to the initial containing block
(viewport).


----------------------------------------
NORMAL DOCUMENT FLOW
----------------------------------------

By default:

position: static;

In normal flow:
- Elements stack normally
- Elements occupy space
- Other elements respect their position

When position: absolute is applied:
- The element is removed from flow
- It does NOT occupy space
- Other elements behave as if it does not exist


----------------------------------------
CONTAINING BLOCK
----------------------------------------

An absolute element searches upward in the DOM
for the nearest ancestor that has:

    position: relative;
    position: absolute;
    position: fixed;
    position: sticky;

If found:
    It positions relative to that ancestor.

If NOT found:
    It positions relative to the initial
    containing block (viewport).


----------------------------------------
VIEWPORT VS ROOT ELEMENT
----------------------------------------

By default, absolute positioning does NOT use
the <html> element.

It uses the initial containing block,
which behaves like the viewport.

If you explicitly set:

    html { position: relative; }

Then it will position relative to <html>.


----------------------------------------
SYNTAX
----------------------------------------

selector {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
}


----------------------------------------
POSITIONING PROPERTIES
----------------------------------------

top     -> distance from top
right   -> distance from right
bottom  -> distance from bottom
left    -> distance from left

These values are calculated from the
containing block.


----------------------------------------
STRETCHING BEHAVIOR
----------------------------------------

If both left and right are set:

    left: 0;
    right: 0;

The element stretches horizontally.

If both top and bottom are set:

    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;

The element stretches vertically.


----------------------------------------
CENTERING USING ABSOLUTE
----------------------------------------

To perfectly center:

    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);

This centers the element inside its
positioned parent.


----------------------------------------
Z-INDEX WITH ABSOLUTE
----------------------------------------

Absolute elements can overlap.

z-index controls which appears on top.

Important:
z-index works only if position is not static.


----------------------------------------
INTERACTION WITH PADDING
----------------------------------------

Absolute positioning respects the padding
of the containing block.

If parent has padding:
    top: 0;

Starts inside the padding area.


----------------------------------------
ABSOLUTE VS RELATIVE
----------------------------------------

Relative:
- Stays in normal flow
- Occupies space
- Moves from its original position

Absolute:
- Removed from normal flow
- Does not occupy space
- Moves relative to positioned ancestor


----------------------------------------
ABSOLUTE VS FIXED
----------------------------------------

Absolute:
- Uses positioned ancestor
- If none → uses viewport
- Scrolls with the page

Fixed:
- Always uses viewport
- Does NOT scroll


----------------------------------------
COMMON MISTAKES
----------------------------------------

- Forgetting to set position: relative on parent
- Thinking it always uses parent
- Using it for full layout structure


----------------------------------------
REAL-WORLD USAGE
----------------------------------------

- Notification badges
- Tooltips
- Dropdown menus
- Modal close buttons
- Image overlays
- Icons inside inputs


----------------------------------------
INTERVIEW ANSWER
----------------------------------------

position: absolute removes an element from
the normal document flow and positions it
relative to its nearest positioned ancestor.
If none exists, it positions relative to
the initial containing block (viewport).


----------------------------------------
SUMMARY
----------------------------------------

- Removed from normal flow
- Does not occupy space
- Uses nearest positioned ancestor
- If none → uses viewport
- Can overlap elements
- Used for small UI positioning

